parts.text:
Now I see an ENDCT3 shape! Probe case 0: `"else\nif x\n"`:
```
source_file
  ERROR "else"
    else "else" (anon)
  ERROR "if x\n"
    if "if" (anon)
    identifier "x"
```

The FIRST ERROR "else" has a NEXT SIBLING = ERROR "if x\n". The next sibling's kind is "ERROR", not a keyword. Hmm, but the plan says "ERROR followed by a block-opening keyword sibling". The sibling is an ERROR node whose text is "if x" — it starts with the block keyword "if".

Hmm. So what does "followed by a block-opening keyword sibling" mean concretely? Options:
a) next_sibling.kind() is one of the block keywords (anonymous kinds like "if", "while"). From the probes, I never saw a next sibling with kind "if" — the sibling was always ERROR containing if.
b) next_sibling's TEXT starts with / contains a block keyword.

Wait — in probe case 0 run 3 (`y =\nif x\n`), there's an ERROR "if " INSIDE an assignment, with next sibling identifier "x". Not useful.

Hmm. Let me reconsider. In probe case 6 (`if x\n y=1;\nend\nelse\n z=2;\nif w\n`):
```
if_statement
ERROR "else\n    z = " (contains else + nested ERROR z)
number 2
; ;
ERROR "if w\n"
```
The ERROR "else\n z = " has next sibling `number 2` — hmm, actually within source_file children: if_statement, ERROR "else...z =", number "2", ";" , ERROR "if w\n". So the first ERROR's next sibling is number "2" — not a block keyword. The last ERROR "if w\n" is last.

Hmm what about probe case 3: `"while y\nend\nelse\nif x\n"`:
